From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] rpmsg: glink: smem: wake the system up on incoming messages

The GLINK SMEM interrupt is requested with IRQF_NO_SUSPEND, so it is
still handled in s2idle, but nothing reports a wakeup event: the message
is received and queued to a socket whose reader is frozen, and the
system goes back to sleep. A QMI indication from the modem (an incoming
call, an SMS on the WMS path, a change of network registration) waits
for the next unrelated wakeup.

On a Galaxy Z Flip3 (SM8350), the phone slept for 2 h 49 min until the
power key was pressed. A vendor QMI service on the modem, which sends an
indication about once a minute while the phone is awake, had sent only
a handful in that time (six were waiting when it woke up), and the
modem had already asserted with "ss_qmi_server.c:1200:Never recovery
from QMI_CSI_CONN_BUSY". qrtr sends RESUME_TX only when the reader
reads, so with the reader frozen the modem's QMI server could not send
any more, most likely until it gave up.

The downstream kernel calls pm_system_wakeup() from the GLINK interrupt
handler for every interrupt after suspend_noirq. Its qrtr also reports a
hard wakeup event, with a 0 ms timeout (CONFIG_QRTR_WAKEUP_MS=0), for
each packet it receives, which covers the part of the suspend before
suspend_noirq.

Do the same for each edge: make every SMEM edge wakeup capable, enable
the wakeup when the edge node has "wakeup-source", and report a hard
wakeup event with no timeout from the interrupt handler. The event
wakes the system up from s2idle, and it also aborts a suspend that is
still in progress, as the downstream qrtr event does. It goes through
the edge's own wakeup source, so each edge is listed in
/sys/class/wakeup and can be turned off in its power/wakeup.

qrtr is not changed: on GLINK, it receives each packet in this
interrupt handler, after the event has been reported.

The RPM edge is left alone. It carries the replies to requests that
drivers send late in suspend, and waking up for them would abort every
suspend.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
Built with W=1 and dtbs_check; not tested on the device yet.

diff --git a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_smem.c b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_smem.c
index 35bb03e..c02a865 100644
--- a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_smem.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_smem.c
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include <linux/of_irq.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm_wakeup.h>
 #include <linux/mailbox_client.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
@@ -212,6 +213,13 @@ static irqreturn_t qcom_glink_smem_intr(int irq, void *data)
 {
 	struct qcom_glink_smem *smem = data;
 
+	/*
+	 * The interrupt is not disabled during system suspend, so that the
+	 * remote can still be served. Report a wakeup event, which wakes
+	 * the system up if this edge is allowed to do so.
+	 */
+	pm_wakeup_hard_event(&smem->dev);
+
 	qcom_glink_native_rx(smem->glink);
 
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
@@ -263,6 +271,10 @@ struct qcom_glink_smem *qcom_glink_smem_register(struct device *parent,
 
 	smem->remote_pid = remote_pid;
 
+	device_set_wakeup_capable(dev, true);
+	if (of_property_read_bool(dev->of_node, "wakeup-source"))
+		device_wakeup_enable(dev);
+
 	rx_pipe = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*rx_pipe), GFP_KERNEL);
 	tx_pipe = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*tx_pipe), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!rx_pipe || !tx_pipe) {
